! Time reference versus viewpoint

Mandarin verbs do not inflect for grammatical tense. Temporal location can come from time expressions, context, and modal/aspectual constructions.

Aspect describes the ''internal temporal structure/viewpoint'' of an event; it isn't identical to past, present, or future.

* 我昨天学中文。 — Wǒ zuótiān xué Zhōngwén. — I studied Chinese yesterday.
* 我明天学中文。 — Wǒ míngtiān xué Zhōngwén. — I'll study Chinese tomorrow.

Neither sentence requires a verb ending for time. See [[Grammar/Perfective 了]] for perfective viewpoint and [[Grammar/Progressive 在]] for ongoing action.

''Nuance:'' 了 has multiple functions, and verbal perfective 了 should not be conflated with sentence-final change-of-state 了.

